## 4. Preflight (run before every build)
1. Overlay all sheets and list every cell still `todo`/`verify` or unfilled.
2. Check every cross-reference: bot weapon/armor ids exist in the shop sheet; sound ids used in rules exist in the sounds sheet; map id and spawn/site cells are filled.
3. Fix those, then build. Build only on a clean preflight.

## 5. Order of work for the agent
1. Connect to Melty, run list_my_mods, search_mashups, game_info for both games, mashup_info skycraft.
2. Locate both installs on the PC (Steam library folders). Read CS2's folder in place; never copy from it.
3. Set up universal-modder and a Fabric dev workspace. Install what's needed from official sources and tell the user.
4. Fill all `verify` cells from the real files, starting with the map and sounds.
5. Build the mod, then run it in Minecraft and capture a screenshot or clip showing a buy phase or a bomb plant on the rebuilt map (game window only, no tokens or private windows).
6. Run inspect_package, validate_recipe, one_click_check (with the feature list above as `says`), create_mod (MIT, remix allowed), uploads, submit_release, add_screenshot, test_release, then summarize and ask whether to publish.
7. Recipe: Minecraft primary with a Prism instance (`mmc-pack.json` naming Fabric, mod in the instance's `mods` folder, recipe.setup so the player signs in once); CS2 companion; no recipe.together.

## 6. Risks to report to the user in one line if they appear
- CS2's map or audio formats can't be read at runtime, so the map or sounds change.
- A bundled library has a license that doesn't allow sharing, so the mod works without it.
- Melty's one_click_check blocks the release, so fix what it lists.
